```java
// Interface — pure contract, no state
interface Flyable {
    void fly();         // abstract (implicit)
    default void land() { System.out.println("Landing"); } // default since Java 8
    // No instance fields, no constructors
}

// Abstract class — partial implementation with state
abstract class Bird {
    private String name;     // has STATE
    private int wingSpan;

    Bird(String name, int wingSpan) { // has CONSTRUCTOR
        this.name = name;
        this.wingSpan = wingSpan;
    }

    abstract void sing();    // abstract method
    protected void sleep() { // concrete protected method
        System.out.println(name + " sleeping");
    }
}

// Can do both!
class Eagle extends Bird implements Flyable {
    Eagle() { super("Eagle", 200); }
    public void fly() { System.out.println("Eagle soaring"); }
    public void sing() { System.out.println("Eagle cry"); }
}
```

```java
interface Config {
    int MAX_SIZE = 100;           // implicitly: public static final int MAX_SIZE = 100
    String DEFAULT_NAME = "App"; // implicitly: public static final String DEFAULT_NAME

    void process();
}

// You cannot modify interface variables:
// Config.MAX_SIZE = 200; // COMPILE ERROR — it's final
```

Every variable in an interface is `public static final` whether you write those modifiers or not. This makes them **constants** — they belong to the interface itself, not to any object, and they can never change.

Why? Because interfaces cannot be instantiated. They have no constructors. There is no 'object' that could own an instance field. The interface exists as a contract at the class level only.

If you need shared mutable state, use a class — either abstract or concrete.

```java
interface Flyable { void fly(); }      // no implementation
interface Swimmable { void swim(); }   // no implementation

class Duck implements Flyable, Swimmable { // FINE — no conflict
    public void fly() { System.out.println("Duck flying"); }
    public void swim() { System.out.println("Duck swimming"); }
}
```

Before Java 8, interfaces had no method bodies. So when `Duck` implements `Flyable` and `Swimmable`, there's nothing to conflict — `Duck` must provide all implementations itself. There's zero ambiguity.

With **Java 8 default methods**, the problem can resurface:
```java
interface A { default void greet() { System.out.println("A"); } }
interface B { default void greet() { System.out.println("B"); } }

class C implements A, B {
    // MUST override — Java forces you to resolve the conflict
    public void greet() { A.super.greet(); } // explicitly choose
}
```

Java handles this by **requiring the class to explicitly override** the conflicting default method. It never resolves the conflict silently.

Interfaces can extend other interfaces to build richer contracts:

```java
interface Readable {
    String read();
}

interface Writable {
    void write(String data);
}

interface ReadWritable extends Readable, Writable { // extends MULTIPLE interfaces!
    void flush();
}

class FileStream implements ReadWritable {
    public String read() { return "data"; }
    public void write(String data) { System.out.println("Writing: " + data); }
    public void flush() { System.out.println("Flushing"); }
    // Must implement read() and write() from parent interfaces too
}
```

Unlike classes, **interfaces CAN extend multiple interfaces** simultaneously. The standard Java library uses this extensively: `List extends Collection`, `Collection extends Iterable`, `SortedSet extends Set`, `RandomAccess` as a marker.

A class implementing `ReadWritable` must implement ALL methods from the entire hierarchy.

A marker interface has no methods — it's purely a type tag:

```java
// java.io.Serializable — the classic example
public interface Serializable {
    // completely empty
}

class User implements Serializable {
    String name;
    int age;
}

// ObjectOutputStream checks: if (obj instanceof Serializable)
// If not, throws NotSerializableException
```

The JVM or framework checks for the interface using `instanceof` to decide behavior:
- `Serializable`: ObjectOutputStream checks before serializing
- `Cloneable`: `Object.clone()` checks before allowing cloning
- `Remote`: RMI framework checks to enable remote method invocation

**Modern alternative**: Annotations. Instead of `implements Serializable`, you could use `@Serializable`. Annotations are more flexible (can carry parameters) and are the preferred approach for new APIs:
```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@interface MyMarker {} // modern alternative to marker interface
```
